The <ansi fg="command">bauble</ansi> command manages the bauble catalog: the
database behind item 900, where every bauble's name, description,
value, weight, where it was found, and whether it was stolen are kept.
Every change reaches items already in the world at once.

<ansi fg="yellow-bold"><bauble></ansi> below is a catalog id (<ansi fg="cyan">B0000012</ansi>) or the name of a
bauble in your pack or on the floor here, matched by any word of
its name (<ansi fg="cyan">bauble show doll</ansi>).

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">Looking:</ansi>

<ansi fg="command">bauble status</ansi>
  Whether finds are on (Balance.BaublesEnabled, off by default),
  what names them (a finder's own key, the server key, or generic
  trinkets), the shared token budget, and the search settings.

<ansi fg="command">bauble stats</ansi>
  Counts by status, tier and namer; unsold value; sales in the
  last day and week; tokens spent; the busiest regions.

<ansi fg="command">bauble list [n] [playerkey|unmoderated|finderonly]</ansi>
  Sales totals, then the newest n records (default 10).
  Only baubles named on a player's own key, those never moderated, or those shown to their finder alone.

<ansi fg="command">bauble show <bauble></ansi>
  One record in full, including who last edited it.

<ansi fg="command">bauble prompt <bauble></ansi>
  The exact prompt that would name it now. Works without a key.

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">Changing:</ansi>

<ansi fg="command">bauble edit <bauble> <field> <text></ansi>
  Fields: name, keyword, desc, material, value, weight, tier.
  Text is checked like a model's answer (no markup, no digits in
  the name, no real item keyword such as key or sword). Value is
  kept inside the tier; changing the tier moves the value into it.
  e.g. <ansi fg="cyan">bauble edit small doll name Rag Doll</ansi>

<ansi fg="command">bauble regen <bauble></ansi>
  Ask the model to name it again, avoiding its current name. You
  are told when it answers; nothing changes if it does not.

<ansi fg="command">bauble retire <bauble></ansi>
  Withdraw its text: it shows as a plain Trinket everywhere, with
  its value kept. For a name that should not be in the game.
<ansi fg="command">bauble restore <bauble></ansi>
  Undo retire.

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">Testing:</ansi>

<ansi fg="command">bauble spawn [cheap|average|rare]</ansi>
  Find one bauble in this room exactly as a search would: it is
  named in the background and arrives in your pack after a few
  seconds. Tier defaults to cheap.
  Values: cheap 1-6, average 10-15, rare 40-200 gold.

<ansi fg="command">bauble window [reset]</ansi>
  Show this room's search roll window (rolls used, when it
  reopens, and whether the zone is excluded), and each feature's
  own window, and whether finds here go to the household.
  <ansi fg="command">reset</ansi> reopens them all.

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">Search:</ansi>
  The chance per roll depends on where the search is (by biome:
  buildings 5%, streets 2 to 2.5%, roads 1%, wilderness 0.25%,
  deep water never; BaubleBiomeChancePct) and on the searcher's
  search skill, which raises it by up to BaubleSkillMaxBonus (it
  doubles at the skill soft cap). A room offers BaubleRollsPerWindow
  (2) rolls, then none until BaubleWindowMinutes (60 real minutes)
  after the first. A find trains search; a miss does not.
  <ansi fg="command">search [feature]</ansi> (a noun, discovered hidden noun or visible
  container) is a full room search plus one roll of the feature's
  own, never from the room's window. The feature's roll comes once
  per BaubleFeatureWindowMinutes (60); sooner, the search takes the
  room's roll instead. Nothing is ever refused, so quests that use
  search on a noun are unaffected.
  Indoors with a resident about, a find stays in the room, on the
  feature, and belongs to the household: <ansi fg="command">get</ansi> refuses it and
  taking it is <ansi fg="command">steal</ansi>, with the steal checks.
  Finds left lying untaken vanish after BaubleUntakenHours (24).
  Instances, banks, storage and character rooms never offer rolls.
  All knobs are in the Balance section of config.yaml; the model
  settings are under Modules.baubles, and the server key, endpoint
  and daily token budget (shared with the AI companion) under
  APIFramework. A player who ticks "Also name things I find" on
  their Companion key page has their finds named on their key. <ansi fg="command">bauble status</ansi> lists the
  table and <ansi fg="command">bauble window</ansi> shows the chance where you stand.

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">Notes:</ansi>
  - Players refer to a bauble by any word of its name, in order
    (<ansi fg="cyan">get doll</ansi>, <ansi fg="cyan">look childs doll</ansi>), or by <ansi fg="cyan">bauble</ansi> / <ansi fg="cyan">trinket</ansi>.
  - Records live under <ansi fg="cyan">_datafiles/world/dogmud/baubles/</ansi>.
  - General stores and jewellers buy baubles for their value times
    ShopBuyRatio; other shops refuse. Sold baubles leave the world.
  - Merchants appraise baubles for free.
